    {
      "id": 22,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read this",
      "gridPos": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 36,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 5
      },
      "datasource": {
        "type": "influxdb",
        "uid": "dmz-mirror"
      },
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {},
        "overrides": []
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**This dashboard reads only the DMZ mirror.** The IT side has no connection to the OT network: the data arrives over **UDP port 8094**, one way, through pfSense (stateless rule, no return path). UDP has no retransmission, so every lost datagram is counted using sequence numbers.\n\n* **Delivery ratio** close to 100 % over hours is the evidence that the one-way link is reliable enough to use.\n* **Rejected** should stay 0. It rises if something in the DMZ sends forged or malformed datagrams (bad HMAC, bad CRC, disallowed measurement).\n* **Lost** rising and then staying flat is a gap that is **never back-filled**, by design of a one-way link.\n* Counters restart from zero when the receiver restarts; the ratios here handle that.",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        }
      }
    }
